{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.two_terms_necessary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.two_terms_necessary","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationSignGateTension.lean#L107-L116","doc":"**Two terms are necessary.** A single term whose high differences alternate —\nwhich is what saturation forces — cannot reproduce the measured cumulants. So\n`b₃ > 0` together with `b₄ > 0` and a saturating binding requires at least two\ncontributions of opposite curvature.\n\nThis is the cumulant-side form of the coordination weight's non-monotonicity\n(`CoordinationWeightDifferences`); the two arguments agree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.same_sign_of_passesSignGate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.same_sign_of_passesSignGate","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationSignGateTension.lean#L92-L105","doc":"**Passing the sign gate forces both differences to share a strict sign.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.sign_gate_fails_of_alternating'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.sign_gate_fails_of_alternating'","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationSignGateTension.lean#L86-L90","doc":"The mirror case, `Δ³ < 0` with `Δ⁴ ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.sign_gate_fails_of_alternating","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.sign_gate_fails_of_alternating","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationSignGateTension.lean#L78-L84","doc":"**A saturating term fails the sign gate.** If the third and fourth differences\nalternate — `Δ³ > 0` and `Δ⁴ ≤ 0`, the shape of every sublinear increasing\nfunction — then the product is non-positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.PassesSignGate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationSignGateTension.PassesSignGate","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationSignGateTension.lean#L73-L76","doc":"The sign gate, as the measurement states it: a standalone term reproduces the\nobserved `b₃ > 0`, `b₄ > 0` only if its third and fourth differences share a\nsign, i.e. their product is positive. ","signatureChunk":0}]}